**Theorem 6.3 (eligible comparison and complete finite realizers).** For
$B=\{r_0,r_1\}$ of (NIS.22), the image-saturation handler applies.
Its unique common closure point is

$$
W=C[K,K,\rho K,\rho K,\rho K,\rho K,\rho K,\rho K],
\qquad K=\langle\rho K,\alpha\rangle.
\tag{NIS.24}
$$

For every supplied $h$, one can produce two distinct legal finite originals
in the entire $D_0,D_1$ sharing the one depth-$h$ prefix of $W$.
Thus $J_B(h)$ holds for every $h$.

Proof. Equality of entire completed source maps first identifies their
two independent coordinates at slots 1 and 2 as $X,Y$. Their selector slots
then require exactly

$$
\rho X=\langle\rho^2Y,\beta\rangle,\qquad
\rho Y=\langle\rho^2X,\beta\rangle.
\tag{NIS.25}
$$

Conversely these equations make all eight whole slots equal. The two
occurrence edges have weight $1-2=-1$, so the only cycle has weight $-2$.
The potentials $s_X=s_Y=1$ give

$$
A=\langle\rho B,\beta\rangle,\qquad
B=\langle\rho A,\beta\rangle,\qquad A,B\in L_1.
\tag{NIS.26}
$$

Both filters pass for every solution of these functional equations:
$\rho B,\rho A,\beta$ all belong to $L_1$, so paired images do too.
There are no free states and no finite parameters. This is an automatic
passing saturation case. Exact inversion equivalently gives

$$
X=\langle\rho Y,\alpha\rangle,\qquad
Y=\langle\rho X,\alpha\rangle.
\tag{NIS.27}
$$

For example applying $\rho$ to the first equation of (NIS.27) gives the
first of (NIS.25), and completed injectivity proves its converse for every
$Y$; the second is identical with names interchanged. The two explicit
pair guards give a unique solution. Swapping $X,Y$ preserves the system,
so uniqueness gives $X=Y=K$, with the already owned guarded continuation
equation in (NIS.24). Its finite prefixes are computable by guarded iteration.

For any requested $h$, apply SLP Lemma 2.1 to the whole $\Delta$, the finite
seed in Proposition 6.2, and the two infinite original-coordinate prefixes
of $(K,K)$. It supplies one finite $(x_h,y_h)$ satisfying all fifteen
negatives and both prescribed prefixes simultaneously. Substitute this
same tuple into both maps of (NIS.23). Nonexpansion and native pairing make
both sources agree through $h$ with the single $W$. Proposition 6.2 verifies
all input predicates and both exact full rows. They are different originals:
at selector slot 3 their values are $A_{x_h}$ and $B_{y_h}$, different by
the same retained $\Delta$. They need not solve (NIS.25) as exact finite
equations.

Conversely any common closure point has the comb skeleton and actual slots
1 and 2. Taking limits of each entire source map and using continuity
gives (NIS.25), and guarded uniqueness forces (NIS.24).
The finite construction gives reverse inclusion, so the common closure is
exactly $\{W\}$. The closure point carries no finite negative or partition
claim. $\square$

